{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on Popper's terms as a purely pragmatic decision rule that does not imply inductive belief in future success, but rather selects the theory with the highest informational content and severest testing history.","counterconditions":["If acting on a theory logically entails an expectation of its future reliability, then the preference smuggles in induction.","If the agent cannot distinguish between 'preferring for action' and 'believing probable', the defense fails."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Pragmatic Selection without Inductive Inference: The agent chooses the best-tested theory not because it predicts future success (induction), but because it is the most informative conjecture available. Acting on it is a 'critical preference' akin to choosing the strongest tool, not the truest map.","optional_refs":["08218abe2d908092f9126b50809338bd6c6cddb0d43384c3b193e668a34006c6","10d9f60e1c1c5582faa984b8051452e4c8ce369fe468d94cbb5f5b0aa2411016"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference for better-corroborated theories inevitably smuggles in induction because any rational basis for acting on a past-successful theory in anticipation of future results constitutes an inductive leap, violating Popper's core prohibition.","counterconditions":["If one can act rationally without any expectation of future success, the argument fails.","If 'preference' can be decoupled from 'expectation of efficacy', the smuggling charge is void."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The Inductive Smuggle Argument: Rational action requires an expectation of future efficacy. Since corroboration is retrospective, bridging the gap to future action requires an inductive principle (nature is uniform/past success predicts future success) which Popper explicitly rejected.","optional_refs":["45959d9c00da89efee4ba1f3e60736076b2d6d6e6221fda42036340eeb2a384a","3637cea364444177831be4afb7ba5959e53607caea36edb8855e811c565e4ca6"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.75},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Popper's position survives only by accepting a 'rational faith' or non-justified commitment to the method of conjecture and refutation, which functions as a pseudo-induction that is psychologically necessary but logically unjustifiable.","counterconditions":["If 'rational faith' is indistinguishable from irrationality, the defense collapses.","If the cost of this stance is the abandonment of rationality in action, the solution is unacceptable."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Rational Faith / Critical Attitude: The preference is not based on logic or induction, but on a voluntary adoption of the critical attitude. We prefer the corroborated theory because we have decided to play the game of science, not because the theory is 'probable'.","optional_refs":["469214066cd0fe367482ab1dbf622f3c2ad7e94d48942d7e905fb1fd4b3ff862","34ad0d33cac00dc9c4d4b87c266edb3c765de3244ddcf992f0423b84911b04bd"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.45},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The problem dissolves if we view scientific preference as an evolutionary selection mechanism where 'acting on a theory' is analogous to an organism utilizing a trait; no induction is needed, only variation and elimination.","counterconditions":["If human rational action requires more than biological heuristic processing, the analogy fails.","If the 'elimination' process cannot account for the specific choice of *which* theory to act on among survivors."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Evolutionary Epistemology Analogy: Just as an organism uses a trait that hasn't killed it yet without 'believing' it will work forever, scientists use corroborated theories. The preference is a survival heuristic, not an inductive inference.","optional_refs":["047ea7b165ebb2b40db2a478a3bf3225602d7b76e333676f368d64b2e8813c3f","5d848ac46e0509ec2b1adfe65e52e96fdfe97e2315ce500bba4be1bfb57964df"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.3}],"simulation_proposals":[]}